Ilmari Karonen wrote:
> Doug Freyburger <dfreybur@yahoo.com> wrote:
>> {
>> 	int     i = rn2(60) + rn2(3*level_difficulty());
>
>> If the expression returns over a hundred a dragon is created.  With a
>> random number up to 60 that leaves 40 from the other half of the
>> expression.  A third of 40 is 13.
>
> A minor correction: rn2(x) returns a number between 0 and x-1
> inclusive.  Thus, the largest value i can have is 58+3*d, where d is
> the level difficulty.  For this to exceed 100, d must be greater than
> 14.  (58+3*14 equals exactly 100.)

59+3*14 equals 101.  It doesn't change your conclusion.  The off by one
errors I made combined to make my conclusion off by 2.  The one you
missed did not make your conclusion off by one.

> Also, level_difficulty() does _not_ depend on your XL in the main
> dungeon: it's simply the depth of the level, with some special cases
> for the endgame.[1]

Thanks for the deeper source diving!

> Thus, dragons can only appear in throne rooms starting at DL 15, where
> they make up about 3% of the monsters there.  At DL 28 -- the lowest
> level where throne rooms can appear, IIUC -- about 30% of throne room
> monsters will be dragons.  The increase is slightly non-linear, but
> not very much so: at DL 21 the frequency of dragons in throne rooms is
> a little over 17%.  (Compare to the expected 15% if it were linear.)
>
> The Castle throne does not count as a normal throne room, and will not
> have dragons, other than the four that are always generated there.

This is a fun detail that I noticed.  The Castle throne is an item
explicitly on the map of a fixed level.  Not the same as a throne room
randomly appearing on a level.

Aren't the Quest mentors on thrones?  Those also are not throne rooms
with court monster generation so no dragons on Quest except the
explicitly generated ones and the healer Quest ones.

> [1] If you managed to dive past a dungeon level with a throne room on
> your way down and only first entered it after retrieving the amulet,
> then, if I read the source correctly, level_difficulty() would return
> the depth of the Sanctum instead of the current dungeon level, causing
> the throne room to be populated with a very high fraction of dragons.
> I doubt this happens very often, at least outside wizmode.

My earlier arithmatic based on random generation concluded that dragons
start appearing somewhere below the VofD depending on the exact depth
and the exact XP level.  I had done that arithmatic trying to figure out
how likely it was to get randomly generated scales once I'd cleaned out
the two castles.  This discussion doesn't change the answer of my
original intent because throne rooms don't appear below the VotD.  It
does change my idea of where dragons can be created:

1) By figurines.  I have not checked the monster distributions of
figurines because if I have a wish I'll go directly to either GDSM or
SDSM.  If I'm wishing for a figurine for a pet it's when I already have
the type of dragon scale mail I want.

2) By polymorph traps starting a depth 8.  Dragons created by polymorph
traps seem to leave scales at the same frequency as monsters born
dragons.  A pet dragon I'll be tempted to ride until it gets killed by
something and only worry about the scales then.  I've had some games
where I survived a dragon form a mine polymorph trap and ended up
wearing some random dragon color for the middle game.

3) In Ft Ludios if it exists in this game.  At Ft Ludios I learned that
a resurrected dragon has a lower chance of generating scales than a born
dragon.  I've wasted two wands of undead turning resurrecting a gray
dragon veinly trying to get scales from it.

4) In throne rooms in levels 15-28 per this discussion.

5) In the Castle guarding the treasure rooms.  In most games I've
gotten to the Castle the chances of scales from those dragons have
ended up more valuable than the treasure in the rooms.

6) Randomly generated at some depth below the VotD.  Since that's below
the guaranteed wand of wishing I'll already have the type of dragon
scale mail I want.  Scales left by dragons killed in Gehennom go into my
collection of "Save the dragon scales, collect the whole set" style
points so they aren't much different from my collections of Nazgul
rings. Where the Nazgul rings appear shallow enough I might polypile
them for other ring types, I don't recall pulling unique dragon scale
colors from my collection to use in a polypile.


